name: root
boss: ROOT
flavor: I answer to nothing. Show me you belong here.
brief: You are standing at a machine you did not build, that you booted from a
brief: stick in your pocket. Everything you need to decide whether to trust it
brief: is readable from where you are sitting.
brief:
brief: This is one argument, in order. Each answer is a link, and the chain is
brief: only worth what its weakest link is worth -- so read what each one
brief: actually proves, and what it does not.
---
setup: Start with the claim. The root filesystem is not trusted because it
setup: looks right; it is trusted because every block read out of it is
setup: checked against a hash tree.
q: %show% that the root is a read-only squashfs on a device-mapper device
a: awk '$2=="/" {print $1, $3, $4}' /proc/mounts
cmp: ref
ref: awk
teach: awk
hint: $1, $3, $4 print the device, filesystem type and mount flags together
---
setup: A hash tree has a single root hash. Everything below it is checked
setup: against that one number, so the whole filesystem reduces to it.
q: %get% the root hash the kernel was given
a: sed -n 's/.*sha256 \([0-9a-f]\{64\}\).*/\1/p' /proc/cmdline
why: live-hw
ref: sed
teach: sed -n
hint: the capture group pulls just the 64 hex characters after sha256
---
setup: That number arrived on the kernel command line. The command line is
setup: baked into the unified kernel image on the ESP, and that image is
setup: signed with keys you enrolled into the firmware yourself. So the hash
setup: is anchored OUTSIDE the filesystem it describes.
q: %show% the whole command line so you can see the hash sitting in it
a: cat /proc/cmdline
cmp: ref
ref: cat
teach: cat
hint: no filtering this time -- the whole line, hash included
---
setup: A checksum stored inside the thing it describes proves nothing -- an
setup: attacker who can edit the data can edit the checksum beside it. This is
setup: the single most important idea on this page.
q: {confirm the kernel halts instead of continuing when a block fails its check|check that the kernel is set to halt, not continue, on a failed block|prove the kernel panics rather than limping on after a corrupted block}
a: grep -o panic_on_corruption /proc/cmdline
why: live-hw
ref: grep
teach: grep -o
hint: panic_on_corruption in the cmdline is what forces the halt
---
setup: Now the other end. Hash the bytes actually mounted and see the number
setup: for yourself.
q: %show% the digest of the filesystem you are running on, and nothing else
a: sha256sum /dev/dm-0 | cut -d' ' -f1
why: live-hw
ref: sha256sum
teach: sha256sum
hint: cut -d' ' -f1 drops the device name sha256sum prints after the hash
---
setup: Compare that against the digest recorded when the image was built --
setup: which you must have carried here from somewhere else. Reading it off
setup: this disk would prove nothing at all.
q: %show% the digest of the running filesystem beside the root hash it is checked against
a: printf 'mounted %s\nroothash %s\n' "$(sha256sum /dev/dm-0 | cut -d' ' -f1)" "$(sed -n 's/.*sha256 \([0-9a-f]\{64\}\).*/\1/p' /proc/cmdline)"
cmp: ref
ref: printf
teach: printf
hint: two command substitutions inside one printf
---
setup: A verified root is worth nothing if code can be added to the kernel
setup: underneath it. Close that door and check it stayed closed.
q: {in one line, report the module loader, /dev/mem and the lockdown state together|report the module loader, /dev/mem and lockdown state together, on one line|combine the module loader check, /dev/mem check and lockdown state into a single line}
a: printf 'modules %s\ndevmem %s\nlockdown %s\n' "$([ -e /proc/modules ] && echo present || echo absent)" "$([ -e /dev/mem ] && echo present || echo absent)" "$(cat /sys/kernel/security/lockdown)"
cmp: ref
ref: printf
teach: printf
hint: three [ -e ] / cat checks, joined with $() inside one printf
---
setup: And nothing writable may be executable, or the read-only root is a
setup: formality.
q: %show% every writable filesystem and whether it is mounted noexec
a: awk '$4 ~ /rw/ {print $2, $4}' /proc/mounts
cmp: ref
ref: awk
teach: awk
hint: $4 ~ /rw/ matches writable filesystems by their mount options
---
setup: One line now. The whole argument, in the order you made it.
q: %show% the root device, its filesystem, its flags and its on-corruption policy together
a: awk '$2=="/" {printf "%s %s %s ", $1, $3, $4}' /proc/mounts && grep -o panic_on_corruption /proc/cmdline
why: live-hw
ref: awk
teach: awk
hint: two commands chained with && so both must succeed as evidence
---
setup: Last. Everything you have learned, aimed at the machine underneath you.
setup: If this prints and the numbers are the ones you carried in, the system
setup: running this command is the system you built.
q: %show% the mounted digest, the anchored root hash, and whether they are being enforced
a: printf '%s\n%s\n%s\n' "$(sha256sum /dev/dm-0 | cut -d' ' -f1)" "$(sed -n 's/.*sha256 \([0-9a-f]\{64\}\).*/\1/p' /proc/cmdline)" "$(grep -o panic_on_corruption /proc/cmdline)"
cmp: ref
ref: printf
teach: printf
hint: three substitutions, one printf. you have used every piece of this before
---
setup: The claim was read-only. Check the flag, not the feeling.
q: %show% only the mount flags of the root filesystem -- the first must be ro
a: awk '$2=="/" {print $4}' /proc/mounts
why: live-hw
ref: awk
teach: awk
hint: field 4 of the / line in /proc/mounts is the flag list, ro first
---
setup: dm-0 is a device-mapper target, not a disk. Something real sits under it.
q: {name the block device underneath the verity root|which physical device is dm-0 built on|show what backs the device-mapper root}
a: ls /sys/block/dm-0/slaves
why: live-hw
ref: ls
teach: ls
hint: a dm device lists what it is stacked on under slaves/
---
setup: Lockdown is compiled in. Compiled-in means there is a file that says so.
q: {print the lockdown mode the kernel is enforcing|read the kernel's lockdown state from securityfs|what lockdown mode is this kernel in}
a: cat /sys/kernel/security/lockdown
why: live-hw
ref: cat
teach: cat
hint: the active mode is the one in [brackets]
---
setup: No module loader means nothing can be added to the kernel after boot.
q: {print present if /proc/modules exists, none if it does not|report present or none for the module loader, by testing /proc/modules|say whether a module loader exists: present or none}
a: [ -e /proc/modules ] && echo present || echo none
why: live-hw
ref: [
teach: [ -e
hint: -e tests existence; && and || pick which word to echo
---
setup: The host's own disks are never touched because no driver can see them.
q: {count the pci drivers named ahci or nvme -- it should be 0|how many sata or nvme drivers are bound on the pci bus|count the ahci and nvme entries under the pci drivers directory}
a: ls /sys/bus/pci/drivers | grep -c -E 'ahci|nvme'
why: live-hw
ref: grep
teach: grep -c -E
hint: -E makes | an alternation; -c counts the matching names
---
setup: p3 counts its own unlocks. Remember the number the way you remember the four words.
q: {print the boot count from the state ledger in your home|read the first field of the ledger file in $HOME|show how many times this state has been unlocked}
a: cut -d' ' -f1 $HOME/ledger
why: live-hw
ref: cut
teach: cut -d -f
hint: the ledger is "count stamp"; -d' ' splits on the space, -f1 keeps the count
